WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:12.029
One computer runs two programs for the coffee-shop payments company: the payments program, and a nightly job that adds up what each shop is owed.

2
00:00:12.267 --> 00:00:19.015
Each runs in its own container, a boxed-off space with its own name, files and list of running programs.

3
00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:23.296
One night, a bug makes the job copy itself, over and over.

4
00:00:24.333 --> 00:00:29.093
Minutes later, the payments program fails too, in a container nobody touched.

5
00:00:29.093 --> 00:00:31.813
It can't start a thread, a new line of work.

6
00:00:32.067 --> 00:00:35.209
How did a bug in one box break the box next door?

7
00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:43.500
It's easy to think a container is a small computer, with its own name, its own files, even its own process number 1.

8
00:00:43.767 --> 00:00:45.752
Let's look at one from both sides.

9
00:00:45.752 --> 00:00:50.073
Here's a container that runs sleep, a program that waits and does nothing.

10
00:00:50.333 --> 00:00:56.377
Inside, ps, the command that lists running programs, shows sleep as process number 1.

11
00:00:56.667 --> 00:01:00.326
Now look from the host, the computer that runs the containers.

12
00:01:00.326 --> 00:01:02.333
What number does sleep have there?

13
00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:07.574
And does the container have a kernel of its own, the core of the operating system?

14
00:01:07.574 --> 00:01:08.544
Pause and guess.

15
00:01:11.767 --> 00:01:16.617
On the host, the same process is number 40037.

16
00:01:16.617 --> 00:01:21.679
Its status file lists both numbers, 40037 and 1.

17
00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:24.893
And the kernel version is the same on both sides.

18
00:01:25.167 --> 00:01:28.463
There's no second kernel and no second computer.

19
00:01:28.463 --> 00:01:31.278
Just one process in a very good disguise.

20
00:01:31.278 --> 00:01:32.995
A container is a process.

21
00:01:33.467 --> 00:01:35.233
So what are containers for?

22
00:01:35.233 --> 00:01:40.989
An old problem: run many programs on one computer, and let each act as if it were alone.

23
00:01:41.233 --> 00:01:44.824
The payments program shouldn't see the job's processes or files.

24
00:01:44.824 --> 00:01:47.741
A memory leak in one shouldn't get the other killed.

25
00:01:47.741 --> 00:01:52.005
And a program an attacker breaks into shouldn't change the computer's clock.

26
00:01:52.267 --> 00:01:59.244
The simplest complete answer is a virtual machine for each program: a pretend computer with its own kernel.

27
00:01:59.244 --> 00:02:04.461
It works, and every program pays for a kernel, a start-up and a slice of memory.

28
00:02:04.733 --> 00:02:08.790
Containers keep one kernel, and use four things it already has.

29
00:02:08.790 --> 00:02:10.400
Picture a block of flats.

30
00:02:10.633 --> 00:02:17.680
The walls and a private letterbox: a tenant sees only their own rooms and post, and can number their door 1.

31
00:02:17.933 --> 00:02:21.439
Every tenant can be number 1, which is great for morale.

32
00:02:21.633 --> 00:02:29.832
Those are namespaces: each container gets its own view, its own process numbers, computer name, network and list of disks.

33
00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:35.257
The meter: so much power and water, and the fuse trips for this flat only.

34
00:02:35.257 --> 00:02:40.204
That's a cgroup: a cap on how many tasks, how much memory and CPU time.

35
00:02:40.467 --> 00:02:44.202
The house rules: hang pictures, but don't touch the boiler.

36
00:02:44.202 --> 00:02:49.141
Those are capabilities and seccomp: what the process may ask the kernel to do.

37
00:02:49.433 --> 00:02:55.052
And the furniture belongs to the building, but what you change is yours until you move out.

38
00:02:55.052 --> 00:03:00.177
That's the overlay: the container's own files, laid over a shared, read-only image.

39
00:03:00.533 --> 00:03:11.356
Underneath, there's still one of each part: one scheduler picking what runs, one page cache, one OOM killer, the part that ends a process when memory runs out.

40
00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:13.781
And here the flats picture breaks.

41
00:03:13.781 --> 00:03:17.501
Real flats share foundations, but each has its own wiring.

42
00:03:17.501 --> 00:03:20.580
Containers share the one kernel, wiring and all.

43
00:03:20.833 --> 00:03:26.792
So a kernel bug that one container can reach is a bug in every container on the machine.

44
00:03:27.033 --> 00:03:32.145
That's why Docker's filter blocks dozens of requests a program could make to the kernel.

45
00:03:32.145 --> 00:03:35.281
When you need a hard wall, you need a virtual machine.

46
00:03:35.667 --> 00:03:37.302
Back to the nightly job.

47
00:03:37.302 --> 00:03:40.299
Both containers are processes on one kernel.

48
00:03:40.299 --> 00:03:44.113
Every copy of the job is a task, and so is every thread.

49
00:03:44.367 --> 00:03:47.089
Nobody put a meter on the job's flat for tasks.

50
00:03:47.089 --> 00:03:52.764
So its copies used up something the flats share, and the payments program couldn't start a thread.

51
00:03:53.100 --> 00:04:01.320
So a container is a process: walls, a meter, house rules and its own furniture, on a kernel it shares with everyone else.

52
00:04:01.667 --> 00:04:02.818
One quick check.

53
00:04:02.818 --> 00:04:06.273
Put a meter on the job's flat: at most 20 tasks.

54
00:04:06.273 --> 00:04:07.856
The bug strikes again.

55
00:04:07.856 --> 00:04:11.599
Who notices: the job, the payments program, or both?

56
00:04:11.599 --> 00:04:12.751
Pause and think.

57
00:04:15.933 --> 00:04:16.961
Only the job.

58
00:04:16.961 --> 00:04:20.597
Its fuse trips at 20, and its next copy fails.

59
00:04:20.867 --> 00:04:25.417
Next door, the payments program starts its threads and never hears a thing.
